[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Headline result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **No behavioral mismatch was found between the Rust translation and the C.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m All 25 tests pass, plus an independent shell sweep of 151 inputs with zero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mismatches. The defect list below is therefore not a list of bugs fixed in the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust; it is the list of divergence classes that were *checked*, each with the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m evidence that the two agree. One real defect was found in the **test suite**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (§6) and fixed there.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 1. `helperBad` returns the address of a local array (CWE-562)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m static char *helperBad()
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     char charString[] = "helperBad string";
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     return charString;      // dangling
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is undefined behavior, so there is no language-defined value to reproduce —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m only what the compiled reference actually does. Verified empirically rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m assumed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Disassembly of `helperBad` in the reference binary ends in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `mov $0x0,%eax; ret` — GCC folds the dangling return to a **null pointer**.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `printLine`'s `if (line != NULL)` therefore fails and the bad path prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   **nothing at all — not even a newline**.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Confirmed stable at `-O0`, `-O1`, `-O2`, `-O3` and `-Os`: input `0` produces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   zero bytes of stdout at every level.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust models this as `helper_bad() -> None`, which reproduces the observed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bytes without unsafe code or a dangling reference. `harness_sanity` pins this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behavior literally so the whole suite cannot silently degrade if the bad path
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ever starts emitting output.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note: this is compiler-observed behavior, not a guarantee. A toolchain that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m actually returned the stack address would print garbage or crash, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `None` model would then be wrong. Verified against the toolchain in use.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 2. `scanf("%d")` truncates; it does not clamp to `int` range
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The subtlest behavior in the program. glibc converts with `strtol` semantics
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m into a `long`, then stores through an `int *`. So out-of-range input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **saturates** at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` and is then **truncated to the low 32
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bits** — it does *not* clamp to `INT_MAX`/`INT_MIN`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Truncation is observable because it decides which branch runs:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input | `long` value | stored `int` | branch | stdout |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2147483648` | 2147483648 | `0x80000000` = INT_MIN | good | `helperGood1 string\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967295` | 4294967295 | `-1` | good | `helperGood1 string\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967296` | 4294967296 | **`0`** | **bad** | *(empty)* |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775807` | LONG_MAX | `-1` | good | `helperGood1 string\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-9223372036854775808` | LONG_MIN | **`0`** | **bad** | *(empty)* |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `99999999999999999999999999` | saturates LONG_MAX | `-1` | good | `helperGood1 string\n` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-99999999999999999999999999` | saturates LONG_MIN | **`0`** | **bad** | *(empty)* |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `4294967296` is the case worth highlighting: a plainly nonzero input takes the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *bad* branch. A translation that clamped to `INT_MAX` would print
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `helperGood1 string` here and diverge. Confirmed by mutation: replacing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `value as i32` with `value.clamp(INT_MIN, INT_MAX)` fails
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `int_range_edges`, `long_range_saturation` and `very_long_digit_strings`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 3. `scanf` reads across newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%d` skips *all* leading whitespace, newlines included — the documented contrast
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with `fgets`. So `"\n\n\n\n7"` converts to 7 and takes the good branch.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered for `' '`, `'\t'`, `'\n'`, `'\v'`, `'\f'`, `'\r'`, individually and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m combined, including a 1200-byte leading whitespace run and a 64 KiB one.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 4. Conversion failure leaves `x` at its initializer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int x = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m scanf("%d", &x);   // return value ignored
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m On either input failure (EOF first) or matching failure (no digits), `scanf`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m does not write to `x`, so `x` stays `0` and the **bad** branch runs. The return
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m value is ignored by the C, so a failed conversion is indistinguishable from an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m explicit `0`. Both produce empty stdout and exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered: empty input, whitespace only, `/dev/null` stdin, `abc`, `-`, `+`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `--1`, `.`, `.5`, `0x` , `- 1`, NUL byte, `0xff`, invalid UTF-8, a UTF-8 minus
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sign, a fullwidth digit, and a UTF-8 BOM.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Only the *first* conversion happens; `%d` stops at the first byte that cannot
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m extend the number and the rest of stdin is never read (`0 1` → bad, `1 0` →
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m good, `0x10` → reads `0` → bad, `3.9` → reads `3` → good).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 5. `SIGPIPE` disposition — a real C/Rust divergence, handled
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A C program inherits `SIG_DFL` for `SIGPIPE` and is killed by it when stdout is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a pipe whose reader has closed (shell status **141**). The Rust runtime sets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main`, which would turn the failed write into an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ignored error and **exit 0** instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src/main.rs` undoes this in `restore_default_sigpipe()`. Measured:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/build/driver              -> PIPESTATUS=141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation/.../release/driver  -> PIPESTATUS=141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Confirmed the test is not vacuous: deleting the `restore_default_sigpipe()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m call makes `sigpipe_disposition_matches` fail (Rust exits 0, C dies on 13).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is the one divergence class where the stock Rust runtime would have been
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m wrong by default, and it is invisible to any test that only feeds stdin and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reads stdout.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 6. Defect found in the test suite: sign invisible without saturation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Found by mutation testing, not by a failing test. Mutating
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `let negative = b == b'-'` to `let negative = true` — treating `+` as negative —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **initially survived the whole suite**.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Why it hid: for in-range values the sign cannot change the branch, because the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m low 32 bits of `-v` are zero exactly when those of `v` are, and the program only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m branches on `x != 0`. The sign becomes observable *only* under saturation, where
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `+huge` → `LONG_MAX` → `-1` → good but `-huge` → `LONG_MIN` → `0` → bad. No
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m saturation test carried a `+` prefix, and the fuzz inputs were capped at 8 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m — too short to reach the `long` range at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fixed by adding `sign_is_observable_under_saturation` (explicit `+`/`-` at and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m past `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`) and `deterministic_fuzz_long_inputs` (10–35 byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m inputs over a digit-heavy alphabet, so overflow and signs interact). The mutant
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is now caught. Verified against C: `+99999999999999999999999999` prints
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `helperGood1 string`, `-99999999999999999999999999` prints nothing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 7. Output formatting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf("%s\n", line)` — the string then exactly one newline, no other spacing,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m no trailing content. glibc rewrites this to `puts`, which emits the same bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Neither program writes to stderr on any tested input; both exit `0`. Confirmed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m non-vacuous: adding `eprintln!("note")` to the Rust fails the suite, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m dropping the `\n` or misspelling the string fails it too.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mutation testing summary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Passing differential tests only mean something if the tests can fail, so each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mutation below was injected into `src/main.rs`, built, and run against the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m suite. All were caught; `src/main.rs` was restored byte-identical afterwards.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | mutation | caught by |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `helper_bad` returns `Some("helperBad string")` (defect "fixed") | 19 tests incl. `harness_sanity` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `restore_default_sigpipe()` removed | `sigpipe_disposition_matches` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | truncation replaced with clamp to `int` range | `int_range_edges`, `long_range_saturation` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | saturation sign swapped (`LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`) | `long_range_saturation`, `sign_is_observable_under_saturation` |
