[38;5;252m[1m## What the C actually does[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two behaviors drive everything, and both are the kind that look like bugs:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mhelperBad[0m returns a dangling stack pointer.[22m GCC folds that to a null pointer ([38;5;10mmov $0x0,%eax; ret[0m — I disassembled it), so [38;5;10mprintLine[0m's NULL check suppresses output entirely. The bad path prints [3mnothing, not even a newline[23m. Verified stable at [38;5;10m-O0[0m through [38;5;10m-Os[0m. The Rust models this as [38;5;10mNone[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m truncates, it does not clamp.[22m glibc converts via [38;5;10mstrtol[0m into a [38;5;10mlong[0m, saturating at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m, then stores through an [38;5;10mint *[0m, keeping the low 32 bits. So input [38;5;10m4294967296[0m — plainly nonzero — truncates to 0 and takes the [3mbad[23m branch. A translation that clamped to [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m would diverge here. The existing Rust already got this right.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The one place stock Rust would have been wrong by default is [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m: the Rust runtime ignores it, so where C dies with signal 13 (status 141), Rust would exit 0. [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m already undoes that, and I confirmed both report 141.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## The real finding[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Since nothing failed on the first run, passing tests were weak evidence. I mutation-tested the suite — injecting 17 deliberate defects and requiring each to fail. Sixteen were caught; one [1msurvived[22m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Treating [38;5;10m+[0m as negative passed the entire suite. The sign is unobservable for in-range values, because the program only branches on [38;5;10mx != 0[0m and the low 32 bits of [38;5;10m-v[0m are zero exactly when those of [38;5;10mv[0m are. It becomes observable [3monly[23m under saturation: [38;5;10m+huge[0m → [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m → [38;5;10m-1[0m → good, but [38;5;10m-huge[0m → [38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m → [38;5;10m0[0m → bad. No saturation test carried a [38;5;10m+[0m, and the fuzz inputs were capped at 8 bytes — too short to reach the [38;5;10mlong[0m range at all.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
That was a defect in my tests, not in the translation. I added [38;5;10msign_is_observable_under_saturation[0m and a long-input fuzz pass; the mutant is now caught.[0m[0m
